      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Blue_Sky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The mountain hosted pioneering cosmic‑ray experiments from 1935 to 1960, earning a historic site designation by the American Physical Society in 2017.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Blue_Sky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1966, researchers used Mount Blue Sky to study altitude training effects on track athletes, a first of its kind.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Blue_Sky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The summit area includes the deepest cirques in the Colorado Rockies, carved by ancient glaciers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Blue_Sky"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Artist Albert Bierstadt first called the peak Mount Rosalie during his 1863 expedition, before it became known as Mount Evans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://mountbluesky.website/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mount Blue Sky rises 14,266 feet, making it the highest peak in the Mount Evans Wilderness and visible from over 100 miles away.",
